## Explicit non-authority boundary

This historical record does **not** establish:

- current institutional, operational, runtime, provider, legal, rights, economic, or certification authority;
- current availability, pricing, eligibility, entitlement, fulfillment, or service levels;
- ownership, license, clearance, privacy sufficiency, security, settlement, or compliance;
- a safe procedure, write path, deployment instruction, or production endpoint;
- permission for an agent, operator, developer, partner, or public reader to act.

If a current page conflicts with this record, do not reconcile by preference. Route the contradiction through the current source-of-truth and supersession workflow.

## Reactivation requirements

The sequence below describes a **new current review**, not continuation of historical authority.

<Steps>
  <Step title="Identify a current accountable owner">
    Name the role authorized to sponsor evaluation. Historical authorship does not transfer current authority.
  </Step>
  <Step title="Reconcile current sources">
    Compare the concept with current doctrine, registry, architecture, legal, rights, security, privacy, finance, accessibility, and public/private boundaries.
  </Step>
  <Step title="Create new candidate records">
    Produce current-version source, scope, evidence plan, controls, rollback, and explicit holds. Never modify history to simulate continuity.
  </Step>
  <Step title="Complete applicable reviews">
    Obtain the required human-reserved and specialist decisions for the exact scope. Validation is not certification.
  </Step>
  <Step title="Verify and promote separately">
    Keep source acceptance, projection, provider deployment, runtime readback, economic proof, and certification as separate evidence events.
  </Step>
</Steps>

<Warning>
  **HOLD — reactivation prohibited.** Historical source existence and documentation quality do not authorize implementation. A newly accepted current source and every applicable gate are required.
</Warning>

## Provenance and restrictions

- Preserve source URI, checksum or immutable identifier where governed, version, author/owner, dates, and archive location.
- State whether the body is public-safe, excerpted, redacted, restricted, copyrighted, licensed, privileged, or culturally protected.
- Do not reproduce restricted evidence, personal data, credentials, unreleased assets, legal strategy, or material without publication rights.
- Distinguish direct source statements, paraphrase, inference, and unresolved interpretation.
- Record known corrections, disputes, takedown requirements, and superseding sources.
- Follow the [Documentation Source-of-Truth & Autonomous Governance](/standards/documentation-source-of-truth-and-autonomous-governance) and [Versioning, Change Control and Correction](/standards/versioning-change-control-and-correction).
